ENVIRONMENT documentation, last updated on 11 Sep 2026

The server can take configuration values from its environment: any quoted
string in ircd.conf may contain ${NAME} references, and they are replaced
with the value of that environment variable while the file is read.

This is the implementation of doc/proposals/004-config-desde-entorno.md.
The C side of it is documented in include/ircd_env.h.


WHY
===

A server that runs in a container does not carry its own configuration.
The server name, the numeric, the link passwords and the oper hashes come
from outside, and the thing every orchestrator can hand a process is its
environment: docker-compose "environment:", systemd EnvironmentFile=,
Kubernetes env/envFrom and secrets mounted as variables.

Without this, the usual answer is to template ircd.conf with sed or
envsubst before starting the daemon.  That writes the secrets to disk, it
breaks on values containing "/" or "\", and a variable nobody set becomes
an empty string -- a Connect block with no password, and a server that
comes up anyway.


THE SYNTAX
==========

  ${NAME}             The value.  Unset is an error: the server refuses to
                      start rather than run with a hole in its config.

  ${NAME:-word}       The value if it is set and not empty, otherwise word.
  ${NAME-word}        The value if it is set, even to the empty string,
                      otherwise word.

  ${NAME:+word}       word if NAME is set and not empty, otherwise nothing.
  ${NAME+word}        word if NAME is set, otherwise nothing.

  ${NAME:?word}       The value if it is set and not empty; otherwise fail
                      and print word as the error.
  ${NAME?word}        The value if it is set; otherwise fail with word.

The ":" forms treat an empty variable as unset, which is what you want:
"IRCD_HUB_HOST=" in a compose file means "I did not set this".

"$$" is a literal "$", and that is how you write a literal "${": "$${".
A "$" that does not start one of the forms above is an ordinary character,
so a password with dollars in it still means what it says.

A default word can itself contain references:

  description = "${IRCD_DESC:-${IRCD_NAME} (${IRCD_NET:-undernet})}";

A variable's value cannot.  What comes out of the environment is copied in
and never looked at again, so IRCD_FOO='${IRCD_BAR}' expands to the six
characters ${IRCD_BAR} and never to the value of IRCD_BAR.


WHERE IT WORKS
==============

Inside any quoted string, anywhere in the file -- including Features
blocks and include statements:

  Features {
          "HUB" = "${IRCD_HUB:-FALSE}";
  };

  include "${IRCD_CONF_DIR:-/opt/ircu/lib}/opers.conf";

And unquoted, where the config grammar wants a number:

  General {
          numeric = ${IRCD_NUMERIC};
  };

  Connect {
          port = ${IRCD_HUB_PORT:-4400};
  };

An unquoted reference must expand to a decimal number; anything else is a
configuration error.  Expansion never produces config syntax: a value is
substituted into the string or number the parser was already going to read,
so whatever a variable holds, it cannot open a block, end a statement or
become a keyword.


AN EXAMPLE
==========

ircd.conf, baked into the image once:

  General {
          name        = "${IRCD_NAME}";
          numeric     = ${IRCD_NUMERIC};
          description = "${IRCD_DESCRIPTION:-${IRCD_NAME}}";
  };

  Connect {
          name     = "hub.example.net";
          host     = "${IRCD_HUB_HOST:-10.55.0.10}";
          password = "${IRCD_LINK_PASS:?set the link password}";
          port     = ${IRCD_HUB_PORT:-4400};
          class    = "Server";
  };

docker-compose.yml:

  services:
    ircd-leaf:
      environment:
        IRCD_NAME: leaf1.example.net
        IRCD_NUMERIC: "11"
        IRCD_LINK_PASS: ${LEAF1_LINK_PASS}

and the whole entrypoint:

  exec /opt/ircu/bin/ircd -f /opt/ircu/lib/ircd.conf -n


WHEN SOMETHING IS MISSING
=========================

  $ ircd -k -f ircd.conf
  Config file parse error line ircd.conf:41: environment variable
  IRCD_LINK_PASS: set the link password

Test a config with "ircd -k" before deploying it: it reads the file,
expands everything and reports what is missing without starting the
server.

At start-up a failed expansion stops the server: the error goes to the log
(LS_CONFIG), to stderr and to opers with the right snomask, and init_conf()
refuses to continue.  On /REHASH the block containing the bad reference is
discarded, exactly as any other syntax error in it would be.

No message ever prints the value of a variable, only its name.  The
environment is where the link passwords live, and config errors are
broadcast to every oper watching.


THINGS TO KNOW
==============

  - The environment of a running process does not change, so /REHASH sees
    the same values the server started with.  To change one, restart the
    server -- which is what changing a container's environment requires
    anyway.

  - The environment survives chroot (see doc/readme.chroot), so a secret
    passed in a variable is still readable where a file outside the chroot
    would no longer be.

  - A variable name must look like a shell variable name: a letter or "_",
    then letters, digits and "_", up to 128 characters.

  - An expansion may not exceed 8192 bytes, and default words may not nest
    more than 8 deep.

  - "ircd -k" and the daemon expand identically; so does every included
    file.


FOR MODULES AND SERVER CODE
===========================

include/ircd_env.h has the C interface:

  const char* env_get(const char* name);
  const char* env_str(const char* name, const char* def);
  int         env_int(const char* name, int def, int min, int max);
  int         env_bool(const char* name, int def);

  char*       env_expand(const char* src, unsigned int flags,
                         struct EnvError* err);

env_str(), env_int() and env_bool() treat an empty variable as unset and
fall back to the default, warning once in LS_CONFIG if the value was set
but unusable.  env_expand() returns memory the caller frees with MyFree().

Modules can call all of it directly -- symbols resolve against the ircd
executable -- and it needs no ABI change: there is no per-module state to
register or to undo on unload.
